This folder does not contain any production keys.

integration-test-signing-key.json is a test-only RSA key pair used to sign the JWTs that
integration tests send to services protected by common-auth0. It exists solely so those tests can
produce a token whose signature the mocked JWKS endpoint can verify.

It is safe to keep in version control, and it is not a finding if a secret scanner reports it:

  - it signs nothing outside the disposable local integration stack, and no deployed environment
    trusts it. Real environments resolve their JWKS from Auth0, not from WireMock;
  - the data it grants access to is synthetic fixture data created by the tests themselves;
  - the private half never leaves the test JVM. AuthTokens publishes only rsaKey.toPublicJWK(),
    so WireMock and every service receive public key material only;
  - the stack it is used against is destroyed and recreated routinely.

It is checked in rather than generated because the key must be identical across JVMs. A key
generated per process meant a Gradle test worker and the local provisioning server published
different JWKS documents to the same shared WireMock, where the most recently installed mapping
wins and the other process's tokens then failed signature validation. See
docs/FRAMEWORK_REVIEW_2026-07-25.md section 2 for the measured behaviour.

To rotate it, generate a replacement with Nimbus and overwrite this file; nothing pins the key
value except the characterization golden, which is regenerated the same way as any other change:

  RSAKeyGenerator(2048)
      .keyID("integration-test-auth-key")
      .keyUse(KeyUse.SIGNATURE)
      .algorithm(JWSAlgorithm.RS256)
      .generate()
